Z||000000||loafer87gt||03-06-2001||01:33 PM||||Fianlly got everything working with my new T-bird and Asus A7V133 MB. I was getting really worried that I had lost everything on my NTFS partitions as they were looking for my old Intel BX motherboard drivers, and didn't seem to like the new VIA ones. After reading through the Ars Technica forum I found that if you choose to do a reinstall, Windows 2000 will detect your old version of Win2000 and attempt to repair it. It successfully detected my new motherboard, and now everything is running rock solid. I can't believe the speed difference over my old Pentium 3 500. An animation that I rendered a couple of weeks ago took 102 hours on my old system, now the program estimates it will take around 32 hours. I have a Alpha fan and a monstrous heatsink, and the machine is running rock solid at 1.4 gigahertz. I'm pretty impressed, with the total cost of upgrading under $200 after I sold my old Intel stuff.||||reg||1
